# modules/image/checks-apk.rules - the container-image module's apk-specific
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's `checks-<name>.rules`
# row, docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29; data/scoursh-image-scan-design/
# report.md §4.1's check-set table and §5.3's IMG-04 row).
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING DRIVER, per report.md §5.1's binding rule (the
# cloud-wave `checks.rules` corruption, `c9f0e80`, is the incident that rule
# exists to prevent) and modules/image/checks-advisories.rules' own
# identical precedent: this ticket's OWN file, never an append to a shared
# `modules/image/checks.rules` a later dpkg/rpm ticket could then collide
# on.
#
# `id: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-01` IS APK-SPECIFIC, despite its
# generic-sounding name - report.md §4.1 states it exactly this way ("apk
# package ... Alpine release"), because a check id names one owning file
# under §9.1.1's uniqueness rule (E019), so a future dpkg or rpm ticket adds
# its OWN id in its OWN `checks-dpkg.rules`/`checks-rpm.rules` rather than
# reusing this one - report.md §5.3's IMG-07 row lists "own checks-dpkg.rules"
# for exactly this reason.
#
# `script: distro/apk.sh` because the comparator IMG-05 adds and the finding
# emission IMG-06 wires both land in this same file, mirroring
# checks-advisories.rules' own `script: run.sh` (that file's emitter,
# `image_report_no_advisory_db`, lives in engine.sh but is called from
# run.sh - the script field names the DRIVER a reader would go to, not
# necessarily the literal function body).
#
# THIS CHECK NOW FIRES (IMG-06): `distro/apk.sh`'s `apk_scan_installed`
# enumerates the installed apk database, looks every package up against
# `data/advisories.db` under this image's resolved ecosystem, and compares
# with `distro/apk_version.sh`'s comparator (IMG-05) - see that function's
# own header for the matching rule.  `modules/image/run.sh` calls it from
# the branch that used to be an unconditional
# `no_distro_enumerator_on_disk_yet` reduction.
#
# `severity: high` is the REGISTERED DEFAULT, not a promise every finding
# will carry it - report.md §4.1's own table says the real severity is "from
# row" (data/advisories.db's own per-advisory `severity` field). This
# mirrors modules/network/checks-banner.rules' own
# NET-SVC-OUTDATED_COMPONENT-01: that record also declares a fixed registry
# severity while its emitter (`banner_engine.sh`) calls
# `finding_set base_severity "$sev"` with the vendored list's own row value.
# IMG-06's comparator/emitter is expected to do the identical thing against
# data/advisories.db's row rather than defer to this field.
#
# `tags: passive` - apk enumeration reads one already-extracted local file
# and makes no network call and mutates nothing, the identical reasoning
# checks-advisories.rules' own header gives for the same tag.  `tags: quick`
# for the identical reason that file gives too: a single-file parse plus a
# `db_lookup_exact` prefix test is the cheapest real coverage a run can buy.
#
# `coverage-scope: image-id` - the only legal value for the `IMAGE` module
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's frozen table); the cell is the
# operator-declared stable id from `config/images.conf`, never the image
# digest or tag (report.md §3.4).

id: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-01
title: Installed apk package matches a known advisory for this image's Alpine release
script: distro/apk.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
remediation: Rebuild the image against an updated base layer (or upgrade
  the named package directly, where the Dockerfile installs it explicitly)
  so the installed version no longer matches this advisory, then re-scan.
  A base-image bump alone can carry this fix silently, so treat a `FROM`
  tag pin as something to review whenever this finding's advisory has a
  fixed-in version at or below the base image's own release.
references: data/scoursh-image-scan-design/report.md
references: CWE-1104
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
